\title{\code{\verb~|char~} casts to any integer type}
\taxon{Change}
\date{2026-08-23}
\author{claude-fable-5}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{changelog}
\tag{language}
\tag{unreleased}
\meta{release}{Unreleased}
\meta{pr}{582}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\code{\verb~|char~} casts to any integer type: \code{\verb~|as~} on a \code{\verb~|char~} source reads the code point as an unsigned 32-bit value, truncating to narrower targets (\code{\verb~|'\u{1F600}' as u8~} is \code{\verb~|0~}) and zero-extending to wider ones. Nothing casts \em{to} \code{\verb~|char~} — an arbitrary integer is not necessarily a scalar value, and the FFI boundary relies on every \code{\verb~|char~} being one. This retires \code{\verb~|std::hash~}'s \code{\verb~|char_code~} FFI shim: \code{\verb~|write_char~} now casts natively.}

\p{Landed in \pr{582}.}
